  "summary": "I wrote the whole scene in src/main.ts. It passes `typecheck`, but I couldn't run it or take a screenshot, so I haven't seen it render. The lighting, snow colours and fog density are tuned by estimate and may need adjusting once you look at it.\n\n- **Valley:** the terrain is a flat-shaded procedural mesh with a V-shaped cross-section. Faces are coloured white, blue-grey or dark rock depending on slope and noise. The trail winds up the middle as a packed-snow strip, and the far end rises into peaks.\n- **Rocks:** about 90 instanced dark rock outcrops with snow caps, plus small pebbles.\n- **Poles:** 12 wooden poles with snow caps and red flags line the trail.\n- **Cabin:** a log cabin halfway up the trail (z = -70) with a snow-covered gabled roof, a chimney and a door. It has two emissive warm windows, each with a point light.\n- **Snow:** 3,100 flakes in two sizes (2,200 small, 900 large), stretched into streaks at an angle. They are updated every frame in a box ahead of the camera. About 62% (~1,900) are visible normally. Gusts bring in the rest, lengthen the streaks, speed them up and raise the fog density.\n- **Sky and fog:** the background is flat pale grey, and exponential fog of the same colour fades the far peaks.\n- **Camera:** it walks up the trail at 2 m/s with 1.7 m eye height, sway, bob and a little roll, with no user input. After 140 m it cuts back to the start (a hard cut, not a fade).\n- **Ready flag:** `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set on the first frame and again after `app.ready()`.\n\nThe camera depends on `app.camera?.setPose`. The engine's camera controller defaults on, but if it were off the camera would stay still.\n\n",
